evidence_text:
ST-ConMa: a multimodal foundation framework for spatial transcriptomics via image-gene contrastive and matching learning. Spatial transcriptomics (ST) enables the spatial mapping of gene expression by combining tissue images with spot-level gene expression profiles, allowing for the analysis of gene distribution and interactions between adjacent regions. Although numerous deep learning models have been developed for ST, most existing approaches are task-specific, and multimodal foundation models with broad knowledge of ST remain scarce. While a few ST multimodal models currently exist, they have been either trained on low-resolution tissue images or developed for a specific platform, thereby limiting their generalizability when applied to diverse ST data. To address this gap, we propose ST-ConMa, a multimodal foundation model pretrained on large-scale spot-level tissue images paired with corresponding gene expression profiles using contrastive and matching learning. While conventional task-specific and foundation models generate modality-specific embeddings for images and genes separately, our model jointly learns image-gene fusion representations using a multimodal encoder equipped with cross-attention and a matching objective. Through extensive evaluations on histopathology image classification benchmarks and domain-specific tasks including gene expression prediction and spatial clustering, we demonstrate that ST-ConMa learns high-quality multimodal representations and consistently outperforms existing methods. These results indicate that ST-ConMa serves as a generalizable backbone for diverse ST approaches. Introduction  Recent advances in sequencing technologies have shifted research paradigms in the life sciences from bulk RNA sequencing, which measures average gene expression at the tissue or cell-line level, to single-cell RNA sequencing [ 1 ,  2 ], enabling precise profiling at the individual cell-level. This shift has allowed researchers to identify heterogeneous cell types and key subtypes involved in disease progression, thereby providing a deeper understanding of pathophysiology and laying the groundwork for precision medicine [ 3 ,  4 ]. A similar transition toward higher resolution has occurred in histopathology, where traditional whole-slide imaging combined with bulk-level gene profiling is being complemented by spatial transcriptomics (ST) [ 5 ,  6 ], a technique that captures gene expression together with spatial context at spot-level resolution. This advancement enables detailed analysis of the tissue microenvironment and interactions between spatially adjacent regions.  With advances in deep learning, various models have been developed for ST and actively applied to key tasks such as tissue alignment [ 7 ,  8 ], cell-type deconvolution [ 9 ,  10 ], spatial clustering [ 11 ,  12 ], and gene expression prediction [ 13 ,  14 ]. However, despite ongoing progress in model development, the lack of large-scale ST data limits most deep learning–based ST approaches to task-specific applications. Such task-specific approaches can typically be applied only to a single disease or tissue type and therefore fail to acquire and exploit generalizable knowledge across diverse ST datasets. Recently, to overcome this limitation and facilitate the development of ST multimodal foundation models, several large-scale datasets containing paired spot-level tissue images and corresponding gene expression profiles have been released. Among these, HEST-1K [ 15 ], STimage-1K4M [ 16 ], and ST-Bank [ 17 ] are representative examples, collectively providing millions of spot-level image–gene pairs across diverse species, tissues, and platforms. These datasets lay the groundwork for training multimodal foundation models that can learn generalizable representations for ST.  Building on such datasets, recent efforts have adopted Vision–Language Model (VLM) frameworks—such as CLIP [ 18 ] and ALIGN [ 19 ], which align image and text representations via contrastive learning using dual-encoder architectures—to develop multimodal foundation models for ST. Among these, OmiCLIP [ 17 ] was pretrained on ST-Bank following the CLIP framework. However, OmiCLIP exhibits several limitations when applied to downstream tasks. Its dual-encoder architecture lacks an explicit cross-modal fusion module, which prevents the generation of integrated image–gene fusion representations and limits the ability to capture fine-grained interactions between tissue images and gene features. Additionally, the use of a broad gene set rather than a predefined gene set shared across datasets, as well as the low-resolution patch images used during pretraining, may further constrain the transferability and generalizability of its learned representations. Similarly, UMPIRE [ 20 ], another recently developed model trained following the CLIP framework with a predefined gene set, relies exclusively on Visium platform data for pretraining, limiting the scale and diversity of its training data (691K pairs). Furthermore, like OmiCLIP, UMPIRE adopts a dual-encoder architecture and is therefore unable to generate fusion representations.  To address the lack of multimodal foundation models in ST and to provide a domain-specific pretrained backbone with a fusion module, we propose ST-ConMa—a multimodal foundation model for  S patial  T ranscriptomics, pretrained on over 981K spot-level image–gene pairs using  Con trastive and  Ma tching objectives. Our architecture is inspired by VLMs and consists of three key components: gene, image, and multimodal encoders. The gene encoder enables the model to capture gene–gene interactions and generate informative gene representations. The image encoder processes spot images to generate corresponding image representations. The multimodal encoder integrates information from both modalities, enabling the model to learn cross-modal relationships between image and gene features. During pretraining, the model is jointly optimized for three objectives: image–gene contrastive (IGC), image–image contrastive (IIC), and image–gene matching (IGM) losses. The IGC loss promotes cross-modal alignment between image and gene representations, while the IIC loss aligns different augmented views of the same image to encourage consistency of image representations [ 21 ]. IGM loss follows the Image–Text Matching objective [ 22 ] commonly used in VLMs such as ALBEF [ 23 ] and FLAVA [ 24 ], and drives the multimodal encoder to learn a fused representation. The combination of these objectives and the architecture enables the model to learn robust intra-modality representations and to capture inter-modality relationships effectively.    Materials and methods  The overall architecture of ST-ConMa is depicted in  Fig. 1 . Our framework consists of three main components: an image encoder, a gene encoder, and a multimodal encoder, which are jointly optimized using three training objectives.   Figure 1   Overview of ST-ConMa framework, in which (a) the model is pretrained on over 981K spot-level image–gene pairs from ST data with three objectives (IIC, IGC, and IGM) for fine-grained image–gene alignment, and (b) the learned representations are applied to histopathology image classification and ST-specific tasks, including gene expression prediction, spatial clustering, and domain analysis.                    Overview of the ST-ConMa pretraining framework and downstream applications. A two-part diagram. Part (a) shows the pretraining pipeline where spot-level images and rank-ordered gene sentences are processed by an image encoder and a gene encoder, respectively, then aligned via IGC and IIC losses. A multimodal encoder fuses both modalities using an IGM objective. Part (b) illustrates downstream tasks including histopathology image classification, gene expression prediction, spatial clustering, and domain analysis.     Pretraining dataset and preprocessing  For pretraining, we used  Homo sapiens  samples from the HEST-1K dataset. The gene name formats in this dataset were inconsistent across samples, with some using Ensembl gene IDs and others including prefixes such as genome assembly references like “GRCh38.” To ensure consistent gene naming, all Ensembl gene IDs were converted to gene symbols using MyGene.info [ 25 ], and then prefixes were removed. Within each sample, duplicated gene names were discarded. We then selected 586 samples that contained at least 10000 genes overlapping with the common gene vocabulary derived from CELLxGENE [ 26 ]. From these samples, we extracted the common set of 7816 shared genes. Subsequently, we extracted only spots containing at least one nonzero expression value among the common gene set, each with its corresponding spot image. For the input to the gene encoder, gene names were arranged in a space-separated sentence consisting of the top 100 highly expressed genes (HEGs) for each spot, ordered from the highest to the lowest expression values. In total, after excluding three datasets reserved for evaluation in the gene expression prediction task—where true expression values are used as targets—534 samples remained for pretraining, amounting to 981K image–gene pairs. These samples encompass data generated using multiple ST platforms, including Visium, Visium HD, and Spatial Transcriptomics ( Figure S1 ).    Image encoder  We adopt PathoDuet [ 27 ] as the image encoder, which is based on the ViT-Base/16 [ 28 ] architecture and captures both local and global features of histopathology images. Each spot image is processed to generate three distinct views: one unaugmented view and two augmented views. The unaugmented view is used to ensure stable alignment with the gene representation, while the two augmented views are employed for IIC learning. The augmented views are generated using random cropping (up to 50%) along with other transformations ( Table S1 ).  
