     Pretraining details  We used four NVIDIA V100 32GB GPUs for pretraining. During pretraining, each GPU processed 32 spots, and gradient accumulation was applied for four steps, resulting in an effective batch size of 512. We employed the AdamW [ 34 ] optimizer with a weight decay of 0.01 and applied a cosine learning rate scheduler with a 1-epoch linear warmup. The model comprises 395.9M trainable parameters and was trained for 12 epochs. Training took   ∼  26 h in total, with peak GPU memory usage of about 31GB ( Table S2 ). The same GPU configuration was also used for all downstream tasks.    Downstream evaluation   Histopathology image representation evaluation  We evaluated the quality of the image representations learned by ST-ConMa using k-means clustering and linear probing on four histopathology image classification datasets: CAMELYON17-WILDS [ 35 ], NCT-CRC-HE-100K [ 36 ], WSSS4LUAD [ 37 ], and MHIST [ 38 ] ( Note S1 ). We compared ST-ConMa with OmiCLIP, a multimodal foundation model for ST; PathoDuet, the image encoder used to initialize ST-ConMa; and an ablation model, ST-ConMa  w/o IIC , which was pretrained without the IIC objective.  For clustering evaluation, k-means was applied directly to the representations extracted from the frozen image encoders. The number of clusters was fixed to the number of ground-truth classes in each dataset, and clustering performance was assessed using Adjusted Rand Index (ARI) and Normalized Mutual Information (NMI) ( Note S2 ). Clustering was performed on the official test split when available. For WSSS4LUAD, whose evaluation splits provide pixel-level annotations rather than the single-class image-level labels required by our protocol, we instead used the single-labeled images from the training split. The number of k-means initializations was set to       \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
