tient may share patient-specific histological and molecular characteristics, this protocol provides a stringent assessment of generalization to unseen patients. For HLT, which comprises four tissue sections from a single donor, we instead employed leave-one-out cross-validation, with one section held out for testing in each fold and the remaining three sections used for training. An example of the data-splitting procedures is illustrated in  Figure S2 .  For downstream adaptation, ST-ConMa was fine-tuned using image–gene pairs from the training fold. The training procedure followed that used during pretraining, except that the IIC objective was excluded, such that the model was optimized using the IGC and IGM objectives. Image augmentation was applied during gene expression fine-tuning, but resizing was disabled to preserve precise alignment between spot-centered images and their corresponding gene profiles ( Table S3 ). The complete optimization settings and implementation details are provided in  Note S7 and Table S4 .  For each test image, gene expression was inferred using a similarity-weighted combination of the expression profiles from the reference set. Let       \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
