ings are provided in  Note S9 .    Spatial clustering  We further evaluated spatial clustering performance on the DLPFC dataset and the human breast cancer dataset [ 46 ]. The DLPFC dataset shares the same characteristics as the one used in the previous section, whereas the human breast cancer dataset consists of a single tissue section annotated with 20 spatial domains. Detailed dataset characteristics are provided in  Note S10 .  For each tissue section, ST-ConMa was adapted using the corresponding image–gene pairs, following the same adaptation protocol as in gene expression prediction, except that image augmentation was excluded. After adaptation, spot-level fusion representations were extracted from the multimodal encoder and incorporated into the STAIG framework. Specifically, the ST-ConMa fusion representations replaced the default ResNet50-based image representations used for edge augmentation, and were also used to generate the pseudo-labels employed for negative-spot adjustment ( Note S11 ).  We compared the resulting method with six spatial clustering baselines: SpaGCN [ 11 ], STAGATE [ 12 ], DeepST [ 47 ], GraphST [ 48 ], MuCoST [ 49 ], and STAIG [ 50 ]. For all methods, the number of clusters was fixed to the number of ground-truth spatial domains in each dataset: five or seven clusters for each DLPFC tissue section, and 20 clusters for the human breast cancer tissue section. To evaluate the learned spot representations without post-processing effects, label-refinement procedures were disabled where applicable. Clustering performance was assessed using ARI and NMI ( Note S2 ). Detailed descriptions of all baseline methods are provided in  Note S12 .    Biological characterization of spatial domains  To investigate the biological characteristics of the spatial domains identified by ST-ConMa, we performed differential gene expression analysis for each domain by comparing spots assigned to the target domain with spots assigned to all remaining domains. Differentially expressed genes (DEGs) were identified using the Wilcoxon rank-sum test, and the resulting   P  -values were adjusted for multiple testing using the Benjamini–Hochberg correction. Significantly upregulated genes were subsequently subjected to Gene Ontology (GO) and KEGG pathway enrichment analyses. Detailed procedures, including the fold-change and   P  -value thresholds, gene-set libraries, and enrichment settings, are provided in  Note S13 .      Results   Image–image contrastive loss improves image representation quality  We first examined whether incorporating the image encoder-specific IIC objective preserves and enhances image representation quality during image–gene alignment. To this end, we compared clustering and linear-probing performance on four histopathology datasets against OmiCLIP, PathoDuet, and the  w/o IIC  ablation. In the clustering evaluation, ST-ConMa consistently demonstrates superior performance across all four datasets ( Fig. 2 ). It achieves the highest ARI on all datasets and the highest NMI on three datasets, with MHIST being the only exception.   Figure 2   UMAP visualization of image representations from OmiCLIP, PathoDuet, ST-ConMa without the IIC objective (w/o IIC), and ST-ConMa on the (a) CAMELYON17-WILDS, (b) NCT-CRC-HE-100K, (c) WSSS4LUAD, and (d) MHIST datasets, with ARI and NMI reported for each model (best in  bold , second-best  underlined ).                    UMAP visualization comparing image representations of four models across four histopathology datasets. UMAP projections of image representations from OmiCLIP, PathoDuet, ST-ConMa without the IIC objective, and ST-ConMa across four datasets: CAMELYON17-WILDS, NCT-CRC-HE-100K, WSSS4LUAD, and MHIST. Each panel shows clustered data points colored by class labels, with corresponding ARI and NMI scores.    On the CAMELYON17-WILDS dataset, ST-ConMa yields substantial improvements over PathoDuet, achieving relative increases of 172.6% in ARI and 127.5% in NMI ( Fig. 2a ). The ablation variant also shows improvements, although these gains are markedly smaller than those obtained when the IIC objective is included. Compared with OmiCLIP, ST-ConMa achieves ARI and NMI scores that are 161.6% and 144.7% higher, respectively. The advantage of the IIC objective is also evident under the linear-probing setting ( Table S5 ).  On NCT-CRC-HE-100K, ST-ConMa again achieves the best clustering performance ( Fig. 2b ). In contrast to CAMELYON17-WILDS, the ablation variant exhibits performance degradation compared with PathoDuet, with relative drops of 8.8% in ARI and 3.0% in NMI, whereas including the IIC objective yields relative gains of 3.9% and 0.9%, respectively. OmiCLIP shows the lowest performance among all evaluated models, with ST-ConMa achieving 45.4% higher ARI and 30.3% higher NMI. Consistent trends are observed under the linear probing setting, where ST-ConMa achieves 15.0% higher Macro-F1 and 15.7% higher balanced accuracy than OmiCLIP.  For WSSS4LUAD, ST-ConMa consistently outperforms PathoDuet across all evaluation metrics, achieving relative gains of 16.6% in ARI and 29.5% in NMI ( Fig. 2c ). Similar trends are observed under the linear-probing setting. In the case of MHIST, ST-ConMa attains the highest ARI, while the ablation variant achieves the highest NMI ( Fig. 2d ). Notably, under linear probing, Macro-F1 and balanced accuracy decrease compared with PathoDuet. However, when a nonlinear MLP classifier is used instead, performance recovers, suggesting that multimodal pretraining produces image representations that are semantically well-clustered but not linearly separable on this dataset.  Across all datasets, the UMAP visualizations further corroborate the quantitative results. While OmiCLIP and the ablation variant exhibit substantial overlap in representations of critical tissue distinctions, such as  Tumor  and  Normal , ST-ConMa consistently produces more clearly separated clusters, indicating superior representational separability. We further evaluated ablation variants without the other pretraining objectives using clustering and linear probing, as reported in  Figure S3 and Table S6 . Taken together, these results suggest that catastrophic forgetting in pretrained image encoders during image–gene alignment can be effectively mitigated by the IIC objective, thereby preserving and enhancing image representation quality.    ST-ConMa enables accurate gene expression prediction for unseen patients  We next evaluated ST-ConMa on gene expression prediction, the task of predicting gene expression from spot-level tissue images. We compared it against six baselines under both fine-tuning and zero-shot settings, focusing on generalization to patients unseen during training. Across all three benchmarks, ST-ConMa achieves the best performance on 10 of the 12 evaluated metrics ( Table 1 ). For the RMSE on the cutaneous squamous cell carcinoma dataset (cSCC) with 250 HEGs and HVGs—where ST-ConMa does not attain the best performance—the zero-shot variant of ST-ConMa achieves the lowest RMSE among all models.   Table 1   Performance comparison on 250 HEGs and 250 HVGs across the HER2ST, cSCC, and HLT datasets, with values reported as mean (std) and OOM indicating out of memory (best in  bold , second-best  underlined ).                     Models  250 HEGs  250 HVGs     HER2ST  cSCC  HLT  HER2ST  cSCC  HLT     PCC   ↑    RMSE   ↓    PCC   ↑    RMSE   ↓    PCC   ↑    RMSE   ↓    PCC   ↑    RMSE   ↓    PCC   ↑    RMSE   ↓    PCC   ↑    RMSE   ↓        HisToGene  0.1367 (0.0888)  3.3108 (0.7198)  0.1861 (0.0400)  
