tor activity ( Fig. 5g ), indicating more pronounced extracellular matrix remodeling.  Taken together, these findings suggest that the left lower region corresponds to a humoral immune niche, whereas the center/right region represents a CAF-dominant immune–stromal niche. More broadly, these results demonstrate that leveraging our fusion representation enables fine-grained dissection of heterogeneous domains, facilitating detailed characterization of distinct microenvironments and potentially revealing novel clinically relevant niches.     Discussion and conclusion  In this work, we propose ST-ConMa, a multimodal foundation model and pretraining framework designed to leverage large-scale spot-level image–gene pairs for ST. By jointly learning image, gene, and fused multimodal representations through contrastive and matching objectives, ST-ConMa provides a unified backbone for a wide range of ST-specific tasks.  Although ST-ConMa adopts contrastive and matching objectives from established VLM frameworks, a key distinction of ST-ConMa is its explicit learning of fused image–gene representations for ST. Existing ST multimodal foundation models such as OmiCLIP and UMPIRE mainly align image and gene representations using dual encoders, whereas ST-ConMa further employs a multimodal encoder to capture interactions between histological image patches and gene features. In addition, the IIC objective helps preserve pathology-relevant visual information during multimodal pretraining. Together, these design choices provide complementary image, gene, and fused multimodal representations that can be transferred to diverse tasks.  Across extensive evaluations, ST-ConMa demonstrates strong and consistent performance. The IIC objective is shown to be essential for preserving and enhancing image encoder performance during multimodal pretraining. In gene expression prediction, the model exhibits robust generalization to unseen patients, particularly under zero-shot settings on cancer-related datasets, suggesting that the learned image–gene alignment is not overly dependent on dataset-specific fine-tuning. In spatial clustering, the fusion representation enables fine-grained characterization of tissue heterogeneity, identifying spatial domains that correspond to known biological structures, including humoral immune niches and CAF-dominant immune–stromal niches.  While the pretraining dataset comprises diverse tissues and conditions, the total number of samples remains limited in terms of inter-sample variability, which may restrict generalization to rare tissue types or disease contexts. As ST datasets continue to grow in both scale and diversity, we anticipate that the effectiveness and generalizability of ST-ConMa will further improve.  Taken together, our findings suggest that ST-ConMa serves as a robust multimodal backbone for ST. By enabling effective integration of histopathology images and gene features, it provides a scalable foundation for the development of future ST-specific approaches and for the discovery of biologically and clinically relevant tissue microenvironments.    Key Points    ST-ConMa is a multimodal foundation model for spatial transcriptomics, pretrained on over 981K image–gene pairs with three objectives.    The image–image contrastive objective mitigates catastrophic forgetting, preserving image representation quality during multimodal pretraining.    The model outperforms other recently developed gene expression prediction methods under both fine-tuning and zero-shot settings.    The multimodal fusion representations enable accurate spatial clustering and fine-grained dissection of heterogeneous domains.        Supplementary Material   Supple_ST_ConMa_revision_bbag535_affiliation_corrected                Author contributions  Taelim Yeom (Conceptualization, Investigation, Formal analysis, Writing—original draft, Writing—review &amp; editing), Dongha Choi (Conceptualization, Formal analysis, Writing—original draft, Writ